.DS_Store

# ══ THE CHAIN IS PER-MACHINE, AND THAT IS NOT A COMPROMISE ══
#
# This file used to say the opposite — `!.aukora/chain.jsonl`, committed on purpose, with the argument
# that "a ledger nobody can read from the repository is not accountability, it is a local log file."
# Half of that is right, and the other half breaks the thing it is defending.
#
# A hash chain links each entry to the one before it. Two machines appending to one file do not produce
# one chain; they produce two, interleaved, and every entry after the first divergence has a `prev` that
# does not match its neighbour. That is precisely the fork condition `verifyChain` exists to DETECT — so
# committing the live chain guarantees the failure the verifier was written to catch, and a merge
# conflict on every landing besides. Two writers cannot share a chain. That is not policy, it is the
# data structure.
#
# So each node keeps its own chain and verifies its own history. Chains are PUBLISHED, never merged: a
# node that wants its record read exports a snapshot under its own name. Cross-machine agreement is then
# a real check — Peter's box recomputing Sam's exported chain and agreeing — rather than an artifact of
# two processes writing to one file and hoping the interleaving was kind.
.aukora/
